---
// /learn/what-is-the-theory-of-data — hosts the deposited **v2.2** source (2026-08-19), byte-identical
// to the Zenodo record: sha256 8a23ffa8…63aecc, md5 d41dbe4b…7038, 28,411 bytes, verified by
// downloading zenodo.org/api/records/22018598's own .md and running cmp — not by trusting a filename.
// Title and subtitle come from the deposit's front-matter; the edition block below is page CHROME, so
// nothing is injected into the hosted markdown.
//
// RE-EDITIONED v1.1 → v2.2 (Huayin's ruling, 2026-08-23, Repair Unit 2). The registry's current record
// for `w-tod-introduction` had been .r05 (v2.2) since 2026-08-19 while this route went on rendering
// .r02 (v1.1) — four deposits behind. Same shape as the two Frame-QL /learn repoints: same URL, new
// edition, superseded editions retained on disk and unimported. v1.1 stays in content/corpus with its
// own consumer row.
//
// THE v4-ERA CHROME IS REMOVED, NOT PRESERVED. The old page carried a dated 2026-08-03 note explaining
// that ToD v4.0 replaced `atom` with `measure`, and a description promising "the bridge to the
// canonical Version 4.0". Both were migration scaffolding built to carry a reader from the v1.1 bytes
// to the theory state of the day. The bytes moved and the theory moved twice more; scaffolding that
// bridges two superseded states is not a historical record worth keeping on a live page, and it taught
// `member` and `series` — vocabulary v6.1 retires and Frame-QL owns respectively. Historical facts
// inside the deposited bytes are untouched; only page-authored chrome changed.
//
// REGISTER (house rule v0.3 §4.3). The deposited body is educational and stays plain. Exactly one
// typed block — "This edition" — carries the adjudicable facts: version, date, DOI, byte-fidelity, and
// the status of earlier editions. The wayfinding line is orientation and stays plain.
//
// The deposit's LaTeX uses \[ … \] display delimiters rather than $$ … $$ — see the edition note in
// the PR; the site's remark-math pipeline is configured for $$ display math.
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { Content, frontmatter } from '../../content/corpus/theory_of_data_an_introduction_v2_2.md';
const DOI = '10.5281/zenodo.22018598';
const EDITION = 'Version 2.2 · 19 August 2026';
---
<BaseLayout title="What is the Theory of Data? — datumwise"
  description="The systematic introduction to the Theory of Data — datums, universes, anchors, measure families and their measures, lawful transformation, and what makes an analytical result checkable.">
  <div class="page-head">
    <p class="kicker"><a href="/learn">Learn</a></p>
    <h1 class="serif">{frontmatter.title}</h1>
    <p class="lede">{frontmatter.subtitle}</p>
    <p class="doi mono">{EDITION} · <a href={`https://doi.org/${DOI}`} rel="noopener" target="_blank">doi.org/{DOI}</a></p>
  </div>

  <article class="prose">
    <Content />
  </article>

  <p class="evidence-note">
    <strong>This edition.</strong> This page reproduces <em>The Theory of Data: An Introduction</em>,
    <strong>Version 2.2</strong>, published <strong>19 August 2026</strong>,
    DOI <a href={`https://doi.org/${DOI}`} rel="noopener" target="_blank">{DOI}</a>, byte for byte from
    the deposit. It replaces the Version 1.1 edition previously served at this URL. Earlier editions —
    v1.0, v1.1, v1.2, v2.0 — remain historical records in the publication registry.
  </p>

  <p class="engage wayfinding">
    Shorter first: <em>A Primer on the Theory of Data</em>, v2.2 ·
    The foundation itself: <em>The Theory of Data</em>, v6.1 ·
    How the language uses it: <a href="/learn/frameql-an-introduction">Frame-QL: An Introduction</a>.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .page-head { width: min(100% - 3rem, var(--measure)); margin: 0 auto; }
  .page-head h1 { font-size: clamp(1.9rem, 4vw, 2.6rem); margin: 0.3rem 0 0.5rem; }
  .kicker a { color: var(--ink-faint); text-decoration: none; }
  .kicker a:hover { color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .lede { font-size: 1.05rem; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .doi { font-size: 0.8rem; margin-top: 0.4rem; }
  .doi a { color: var(--ink-faint); }
  .evidence-note { width: min(100% - 3rem, var(--measure)); margin: 3rem auto 0; padding-top: 1.5rem;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); font-size: 0.9rem; color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .engage { width: min(100% - 3rem, var(--measure)); margin: 1.5rem auto 0;
    font-size: 0.92rem; color: var(--ink-soft); }
</style>
